    public void test() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

interface A {

    default void test() {
        System.out.println("Interface");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent implements A {
}
```

Then:

```java
new Child().test();
```

Output:

```text
Parent
```

Because:

> Class implementation takes precedence over interface default implementation.

